% ============================================================================
% sound-device-type — two named sound devices and what each actually does
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Writers reach for sound, not just meaning, to make language memorable: an
% onomatopoeia is a word that IS the sound it names, alliteration is the
% SAME consonant sound repeated across nearby words. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? sound_device_type(onomatopoeia, $D)
%       % is_when_a_word_imitates_the_natural_sound_of_a_thing
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `sound_device_type(device, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a device that is not one of these two. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the device it
% names:
%
%     ? sound_device_type($D, repeating_consonant_sounds_right_next_to_each_other)
%       % alliteration
%
% The two devices, as a little truth table:
%
%     device          description                                                          quote (verbatim)
%     --------------  -------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     onomatopoeia     is_when_a_word_imitates_the_natural_sound_of_a_thing                  "Onomatopoeia is when a word imitates the natural sound of a thing, making the description more expressive and vivid."
%     alliteration     repeating_consonant_sounds_right_next_to_each_other                   "Alliteration refers to repeating consonant sounds right next to each other, which creates a memorable or melodic effect."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "20 Types of Figures of
% Speech: Definitions and Examples" article, which lists twenty devices in
% total. WebFetch-verified before writing (twice — the second pass pulled
% the full surrounding paragraph for each candidate sentence, plus the
% complete list of all twenty device headings on the page, to confirm each
% target sentence stands alone grammatically and to check whether any
% OTHER device on the page also had an equally clean single defining
% sentence). Only these two — onomatopoeia and alliteration — carry a
% genuinely standalone, complete defining sentence; the article's other
% eighteen devices are each introduced through a definition that leans on
% a following example sentence, a comparison to a neighboring device, or
% multiple clauses, rather than one clean single-fact sentence like the
% two tabled here. `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable secondary
% reference, the same tier `figurative-language-type.adj` (a sibling
% library in this directory, sourced from a DIFFERENT Grammarly article)
% already uses. A figure of speech that is NOT one of these two — "simile",
% a real device this same page also covers with its own write-up — is
% deliberately NOT a row: simile is already grounded as its own
% separately-shipped library in this directory (`simile-meaning.adj`), so
% tabling it again here under a different predicate would duplicate
% coverage rather than add to it. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table sound_device_type {
    columns device, description

    row (onomatopoeia, is_when_a_word_imitates_the_natural_sound_of_a_thing)
    row (alliteration, repeating_consonant_sounds_right_next_to_each_other)

    source "Onomatopoeia is when a word imitates the natural sound of a thing, making the description more expressive and vivid."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/literary-devices/figure-of-speech/"
    trust consensus
}
